01 | The Six Rival Kingdoms Were Gone. Why Did China’s First Emperor Still Care About a Merchant’s Scale?

After Qin Shi Huang became the first emperor to unify China, he no longer had any rival kings left to defeat.

The states of Qi, Chu, Yan, Han, Zhao, and Wei were gone as independent powers. For the first time, one ruler claimed authority over the whole political world that had once been divided among them.

If the story ended there, the next step should have been celebration.

Instead, the emperor began worrying about things that looked surprisingly small.

One of them was the scale itself.

What, exactly, did one standard unit of weight mean?

That sounds strange. Why would a ruler who had just finished decades of war care about a merchant’s scale?

The answer is simpler than it looks.

Before unification, the old states had developed their own habits and standards. A unit of length in one place might not match the unit used somewhere else. The same was true of measures for grain and other goods.

As long as the states were separate, that was inconvenient but manageable. Merchants could convert. Officials could negotiate. Everyone already knew they were moving between different systems.

But once the empire claimed to be one political unit, the old differences became a new kind of problem.

Grain had to be moved across former borders.

Taxes had to be assessed.

Soldiers had to be supplied.

Roads had to be built.

Officials had to keep accounts.

Merchants had to travel farther than before.

At that point, a scale was no longer just a merchant’s tool.

If the capital ordered grain by one measure, but the same unit meant a different quantity across an old border, the arithmetic of empire began to break down.

The same problem appeared when taxes were calculated by one standard and delivered by another.

The six kingdoms had disappeared from the map, but their invisible borders had not disappeared from daily life.

They were still hiding in scales.

In rulers.

In grain measures.

In the way people bought, sold, taxed, and counted.

So the emperor began regulating those small things.

Because making a huge territory operate as one place requires more than painting it the same color on a map.

Eventually, people have to weigh with the same scale, measure with the same ruler, and keep accounts that mean the same thing on both sides of the old border.

Winning six kingdoms and making six kingdoms function together turned out to be two different problems.

