02 | Why Did China’s First Emperor Care How Far Apart a Cart’s Wheels Were?

After unifying China, Qin Shi Huang did something that sounds almost absurdly specific.

He standardized the track width used by carts.

Why should an emperor care how a cart moved?

Premodern roads were not smooth asphalt surfaces. Carts repeatedly traveling along the same route could wear deep ruts into the ground. If different regions used vehicles built to different widths, their wheels did not fit the same ruts.

A cart that moved perfectly well at home could become awkward once it entered another region.

When the old kingdoms were still separate, that was not surprising.

A cart from Zhao and a cart from Chu belonged to different political worlds. Nobody expected their roads, measures, or habits to fit neatly together.

But once the kingdoms were gone, the emperor wanted officials, soldiers, grain convoys, messages, and merchants to move across a much larger space.

Now the old local differences became friction.

This is where conquest and integration begin to look different.

An army can cross an unfamiliar road once.

It can bring engineers.

It can repair a bridge.

It can improvise.

But a large state cannot rely on military improvisation every time an ordinary cart crosses a former border.

Daily movement has to become routine.

Roads have to connect.

Vehicles have to fit.

Goods have to travel.

Officials have to reach the places they govern.

The most interesting thing about standardizing cart tracks is not that the emperor wanted to control every wheel in the empire.

It is that political unification eventually reaches down into very ordinary objects.

A map can change in one war.

Everyday boundaries take much longer to erase.

Sometimes they survive in something as small as two wheels and the distance between them.

