03 | Why Would a Ruler Rather Appoint a Replaceable Magistrate Than Give the Land to His Own Relatives?

Suppose an ancient ruler conquers a new territory.

What is the easiest way to govern it?

Give it to someone you trust.

A son.

A brother.

A relative.

A loyal general.

That is an extremely natural solution. The new lord governs the place, keeps order, collects resources, and provides soldiers when needed. The ruler at the center does not have to manage every village himself.

Early Zhou China used a system built heavily around this kind of enfeoffment.

But land changes people over time.

The first generation may remember exactly who granted the territory.

The second probably remembers too.

By the fifth or tenth generation, the story can feel very different.

The land is no longer “a place the king asked our family to govern.”

It becomes “our family’s land.”

The soldiers are ours.

The local taxes are ours.

The officials are ours.

The people living there know our family, not some distant king.

A man who began as the ruler’s agent can slowly become the ruler’s problem.

By the Warring States period, another method had become increasingly important: the county.

A county magistrate was not the hereditary owner of the territory.

He was an official.

He could be appointed today and transferred tomorrow.

If he failed, he could be removed.

When he died, his son did not automatically inherit the office.

That may sound like a minor administrative detail.

It was not.

If a place is entrusted to a family forever, the place can gradually become part of that family’s private political world.

If it is governed by an official who can be reassigned, the territory remains more clearly attached to the larger state.

So the interesting question is not simply whether one system was “centralized” and the other “feudal.”

The more basic question is this:

When a man governs a place, is he running it for the state—or building something his children will one day call their own?

