04 | Why Did Shang Yang Offer a Huge Reward Just to Move a Piece of Wood?

Shang Yang was a reformer who helped transform the state of Qin during the Warring States era.

Before launching major reforms, he is said to have staged a strange little experiment.

A wooden pole was placed near one of the city gates.

The announcement was simple: anyone who moved it to another gate would receive a reward.

People did not believe it.

Why would the government pay that much money for moving a piece of wood?

Nobody moved.

So the reward was raised.

Eventually someone tried.

The pole was carried to the other gate.

And the reward was paid.

The story is often remembered in China as “establishing trust by moving a pole.”

But the interesting question is why a reformer preparing to change an entire state would begin with something so trivial.

Because a law can fail long before anyone openly resists it.

People can hear an order and simply assume it will not really be enforced.

They may have seen promises ignored, punishments negotiated, rewards delayed, or rules bent for the well connected.

In that kind of environment, an impressive law code can still be just paper.

The wooden pole tested something more basic than obedience.

Did the government mean what it said?

The person moved the pole.

The money was paid.

What the crowd saw was not merely a strange reward.

They saw a signal.

This time, perhaps, words would be followed by action.

New institutions are fragile at the beginning.

They have no habit behind them.

No long memory.

No automatic authority.

Sometimes they first have to prove themselves through something almost embarrassingly small.

Shang Yang chose a piece of wood.

Before changing the behavior of millions, he first had to convince a few dozen people standing near a city gate that the new rules were real.

