05 | Why Did Qin Suddenly Want to Know Exactly How Many People Lived in Your House?

Why would an ancient state care about household registration?

Today that sounds normal.

More than two thousand years ago, it was expensive and difficult.

How many households are in this village?

How many people live in each one?

Who is an adult?

Who can serve in the army?

Who owns land?

Who moved away?

Who just arrived?

Someone has to record all of that.

Then someone has to update it.

A state that depends mainly on hereditary nobles does not necessarily need to know every village in such detail.

It can ask the noble to deliver soldiers and taxes and leave much of the local administration to him.

But late Warring States warfare changed the calculation.

Armies grew larger.

Wars lasted longer.

Mobilization became heavier.

Hundreds of thousands of soldiers do not survive on courage.

They need food.

Replacements.

Taxes.

Transport.

Weapons.

A state that does not know how many people, fields, and taxable households it controls cannot sustain that kind of war for long.

That is why the ordinary farmer suddenly mattered to the state in a new way.

He was not just a man living in a village.

He was also a worker on the land, a possible soldier, a source of tax, and a number in the government’s calculations.

The state’s vision began moving downward.

From aristocrats to villages.

From villages to households.

From households to individual people.

That is what makes household registration so interesting.

A person who once belonged mainly to a family and a local community began appearing directly in the state’s records.

The government no longer knew only that “there is a village here.”

It began to know who lived inside it.

