08 | Why Did the State of Han Help Qin Build a Canal That Eventually Made Qin Stronger?

Late in the Warring States era, the state of Han faced a dangerous problem.

Qin, its powerful western rival, was becoming stronger.

Defeating Qin directly was difficult.

So, according to the traditional account, Han tried something more indirect.

An engineer named Zheng Guo was sent to Qin to promote a huge irrigation project.

The idea was clever.

Large construction projects are expensive.

A canal requires labor, food, tools, money, and years of effort.

If Qin could be persuaded to pour resources into digging one, perhaps it would have fewer resources available for war.

In other words: make your enemy spend himself tired.

But the plan produced the opposite result.

The canal was built.

Instead of weakening Qin, it improved irrigation in parts of the Guanzhong plain, Qin’s agricultural heartland.

More reliable irrigation meant more productive land.

More productive land meant more grain.

And more grain made a war-making state even harder to exhaust.

A project that was supposed to drain Qin’s strength ended up adding to it.

That is what makes the Zhengguo Canal story so memorable.

The basic mistake was easy to make.

During construction, the canal looked like pure cost.

Workers were not fighting.

Food had to be supplied to the work site.

Money and materials disappeared into earth and mud.

But once the canal existed, the calculation changed.

The workers went home.

The construction phase ended.

The water kept flowing.

The fields kept benefiting.

What had looked like a temporary burden became a long-lived productive asset.

So the most interesting part of the story is not simply that Han’s plan failed.

It is the deeper practical lesson hiding inside the failure:

You cannot judge an expensive project only by what it consumes while it is being built.

You also have to ask what remains after the workers leave—and who becomes more capable because it remains.

