09 | Why Was Qin So Obsessed With Farming?

The state of Qin had a strong preference for agriculture.

It cared about fields.

Grain.

Farmers.

Cultivation.

From a modern distance, that can sound almost boring.

If a state wants to become powerful, should it not focus first on weapons?

The answer is that ancient war consumed far more than swords.

It consumed food.

An army eats every day.

Horses eat.

Transport workers eat.

Laborers repairing roads eat.

Garrison troops eat.

A battle may last a day.

A war can last a year.

Weapons can be stored in advance.

Food disappears every time someone takes a meal.

That means the real limit on prolonged warfare is often not how many spears a state can forge.

It is how long the state can keep feeding everyone who carries them.

This is why Warring States governments became increasingly interested in land, households, taxes, granaries, and agricultural output.

Those things are less dramatic than cavalry and chariots.

But a chariot campaign that reaches its third month still has to stop if the food does not arrive.

Qin’s emphasis on agriculture was therefore not simply a moral preference for farming.

Agriculture was part of the war machine.

A general decided how to fight a battle.

Food decided whether the war could continue.

Sometimes the most frightening thing about a powerful state is not the sharpness of its weapons.

It is the number of people behind those weapons who can keep sending another meal forward tomorrow.

