10 | Why Was the Battle of Changping About More Than One Bad General?

When Chinese readers hear the name Changping, many immediately think of Zhao Kuo.

He is associated with the famous story of “discussing war on paper”—a commander remembered as someone who understood military theory better than battlefield reality.

The story is so famous that one of the largest wars of the Warring States era is often reduced to one man making a terrible mistake.

But wars involving enormous armies cannot be explained by personality alone.

Hundreds of thousands of men have to eat every day.

They need weapons.

Replacements.

Orders.

Transport.

Medical care.

And while all of this is happening, the rear areas still have to keep producing enough that the state itself does not collapse before the front line does.

That changes the nature of the contest.

It is no longer only about who has the cleverer commander.

It is also about who can keep the war going.

Zhao was not a weak state.

It had strong armies and capable generals.

But once a war becomes prolonged, states begin competing through everything behind the battlefield:

grain,

population,

roads,

mobilization,

finance,

and the ability of the home front to endure.

At that point, war looks less like two warriors fighting a duel.

It looks more like two large machines being pushed toward their limits to see which begins to break first.

Commanders still matter enormously.

A bad decision can be catastrophic.

But if we look only at Zhao Kuo, we miss the stranger part of Changping.

The fact that so many men could face one another for so long already tells us that this was no longer just a battle between generals.

Two entire states had brought their bodies to the battlefield.

