12 | The Wars Were Over. Why Did Qin Shi Huang Keep Building Roads?

Why would an emperor who had just unified China spend so much effort building roads?

The easy answer is travel.

Qin Shi Huang did travel widely.

But imperial travel alone does not explain the scale of the problem.

A newly unified state needed roads because almost everything important had to move.

Orders had to leave the capital.

Officials had to reach new posts.

Armies had to redeploy.

Grain had to move.

Frontiers had to be supplied.

Reports had to return from distant places.

During a war, a road can be a temporary military route.

After unification, the same route becomes something else.

It becomes part of the daily circulation of the state.

That is why roads are easy to overlook and hard to replace.

They are like blood vessels.

Most of the time you barely notice them.

But once one is blocked, a distant local problem can quickly become a problem for the whole body.

Qin’s road building therefore reveals something that conquest alone could not solve.

Making the map one color did not automatically make the territory connected.

If people could not move, grain could not move, and orders could not move, then “unification” remained partly a political claim.

A huge state becomes real through very ordinary things.

Roads.

Carts.

Relay stations.

Granaries.

The grandest empire is eventually tested by a very plain question:

Can the thing that needs to arrive actually get there?

