14 | Qin Collapsed. Why Didn’t the County Magistrate Disappear With It?

The Qin dynasty did not last long.

From the final unification of the rival states to the dynasty’s collapse, only a little more than a decade passed.

A political system that fails that quickly might be expected to take its institutions down with it.

But that is not what happened.

The Han dynasty that followed Qin did not simply return China to the older Zhou model of hereditary lords ruling most of the land as permanent family possessions.

The early Han did restore and preserve some regional kingdoms.

But counties and commanderies remained important.

Over time, direct administration by the central state became even more important.

That is puzzling.

If Qin institutions were nothing more than the personal obsession of Qin Shi Huang, why did so many of them survive after both the emperor and his dynasty were gone?

One likely answer is very practical.

Some institutions solve problems that do not disappear when the people who created them die.

A county magistrate can be replaced.

The district he governs is not automatically his family property.

The central state can appoint officials, collect taxes, apply law, and maintain a more direct relationship with local administration.

For any ruler trying to manage a huge territory, those tools are extremely useful.

Liu Bang, the rebel leader who founded the Han dynasty, could dislike Qin rule.

Ordinary people could hate Qin’s harshness.

But once a new government sat in the same position, it encountered many of the same practical problems:

How do you actually govern a territory this large?

That is where history becomes interesting.

Qin died.

But some of the solutions forced into existence during the Qin period did not die with it.

A dynasty can fail.

A tool that has proved useful may survive the people who invented it.

