15 | Liu Bang Won the Empire. Why Didn’t He Simply Turn China Back Into the Warring States?

After Liu Bang defeated Xiang Yu and founded the Han dynasty, he faced a practical question.

Qin was gone.

Xiang Yu was defeated.

What should the political map look like now?

One obvious answer would have been to reverse Qin completely.

Let regional lords govern their own territories.

Divide the land among powerful families and allies.

Keep only a loose central authority above them.

That would not have been an absurd idea.

Qin’s highly centralized system had just collapsed in spectacular fashion.

Many people had every reason to believe that the safest response was to abandon it.

But the Han dynasty did not fully return to the old world.

At first, it used a mixed arrangement.

There were regional kingdoms.

There were also centrally administered commanderies and counties.

Local princes remained powerful.

But so did officials appointed by the central government.

Over time, direct central administration became stronger rather than weaker.

Why?

Because Qin had failed, but the problems that had shaped the Warring States world had not disappeared.

The land was still there.

The population was still there.

Taxes still had to be collected.

Frontiers still had to be defended.

Roads still had to be maintained.

Regional powers could still grow strong enough to challenge the center.

If the empire were permanently divided into hereditary political units with their own armies and interests, many of the old conflicts could return.

So Liu Bang was not facing a simple choice between “Qin was good” and “Qin was bad.”

He faced a much harder question.

Many Qin practices had been too harsh.

But many of the problems Qin had been trying to solve were real.

That may be one of history’s most common traps.

When an old system fails, the new one cannot always survive by doing the exact opposite.

Some mistakes can be discarded.

Some problems are still waiting exactly where they were.


